        <div id="types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Lithium Jump Starter or Jump Box: Which Portable Jump Starter Do You Need?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every jump starter does the same basic job: it stores enough current to turn your starter motor over when the car battery cannot. People call them jump starters, jump boxes, jump packs and battery jump starters, and the names are used interchangeably. What actually differs is the battery inside and what else is built in.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Lithium Jump Packs</h3>

          <p>
            Most portable jump starters sold today, including the NOCO, Hulkman, TOPDON, GOOLOO and AstroAI picks below, use lithium batteries. They are small enough for a glove box or under a seat, hold their charge for months, and usually add a USB power bank and a flashlight. The smaller ones weigh between about one and three pounds, according to their listings.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Lead-Acid Jump Boxes</h3>

          <p>
            The older style of jump box, like the Clore Jump-N-Carry, uses a sealed lead-acid battery. These are heavier and bulkier but often come with long, thick cables, which is why they remain common in repair shops and tow trucks. They do best kept on a charger in a garage rather than rattling around in a trunk for months.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Jump Starters With Air Compressors</h3>

          <p>
            Combination units like the GOOLOO A3 add a <a href="/best-cordless-tire-inflator/">cordless tire inflator</a>. That makes sense if you do not already carry an inflator, since cold weather drains batteries and lowers tire pressure on the same mornings. The trade-off is size: a combo is bulkier than a jump-only pack.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Smart Clamps and Safety Features</h3>

          <p>
            Almost every lithium pack now uses smart clamps or a control module that only sends power when the clamps are connected correctly. NOCO calls it spark-proof and reverse polarity protection; others list 10 protections. This is the main reason a modern jump starter is easier and safer for most drivers than jumper cables and a second car.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="amps" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Peak Amps vs Cranking Amps: How Much Jump Starter Do You Need?
          </h2>

          <p>
            The biggest number on every jump starter box is peak amps, and it is the least useful figure to shop by. Here is how to read the ratings and pick the right size.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What Peak Amps Means</h3>

          <p>
            Peak amps is the maximum current a pack can deliver for an instant. It is not a standardized test, and makers measure it in different ways, which is why a small pack can claim 4,000 amps while a heavy-duty unit claims 3,000. Two packs with the same peak rating can perform very differently on a cold engine.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Cranking Amps: The Number Most Listings Leave Out</h3>

          <p>
            What turns an engine over is the current a pack can sustain for several seconds, closer to cranking amps. Few lithium listings give it. TOPDON is an exception: it lists 3,000 peak amps for the JS3000 and adds 750 amps for 2 seconds. Do not confuse either figure with the cold cranking amps printed on your car battery, which is a different, standardized rating for the battery itself.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Shop by the Engine-Size Rating Instead</h3>

          <p>
            The most practical number is the engine rating the maker gives, such as up to 6.0 liters gas and 3.0 liters diesel for the NOCO GB40 and AstroAI S8, or up to 9.0 liters gas and 7.0 liters diesel for the NOCO GB150 and TOPDON JS3000. Find your engine size in your owner's manual and choose a pack rated above it, with some headroom if you live somewhere cold.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Diesel Engines Need a Bigger Pack</h3>

          <p>
            Diesels have high compression and often large batteries, so every maker gives a lower diesel rating than gas rating. A pack rated for 6.0 liters gas may only be rated for 3.0 liters diesel. If you drive a diesel pickup, look at the diesel number first.
          </p>

